      "title": "CM Suvendu Adhikari defends EC, blames Mamata for deletions in Bengal SIR",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/politics/cm-suvendu-adhikari-defends-ec-blames-mamata-for-deletions-in-bengal-sir-126092700381_1.html",
      "description": "West Bengal Chief Minister Suvendu Adhikari on Sunday mounted a strong defence of the Election Commission amid an escalating political row over the Special Intensive Revision of electoral rolls, blaming former CM Mamata Banerjee for the deletion of 27 lakh names and asserting that neither the BJP nor the poll panel could be held responsible for the exclusions.\nAddressing a press conference here, Adhikari also seized on Saturday's meeting of Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar and Election Commissioners Sukhbir Singh Sandhu and Vivek Joshi, saying the meeting and the subsequent statement issued by the poll panel made it clear that decisions relating to the SIR were not taken unilaterally by the CEC.\nShowing a photograph of the CEC and the two election commissioners sitting together, as released by the poll panel a day ago, the BJP leader said the meeting of the three proved that the decisions were taken collectively.\nThe Election Commission said on Saturday that the June 24 ord",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-27T14:42:23"
    },
    {
      "title": "Wangchuk backs CJP's Oct 2 protest against CEC, may not attend physically",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/wangchuk-backs-cjp-s-oct-2-protest-against-cec-may-not-attend-physically-126092700350_1.html",
      "description": "Activist Sonam Wangchuk on Sunday backed the Cockroach Janta Party's proposed October 2 protest seeking accountability from Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar, saying efforts to improve accountability in the country and the electoral process deserved support.\nWhile he expressed his support for CJP's proposed agitation, Wangchuk added that he might not be physically present during the protest.\nHis comments came amid a row over the functioning of the Election Commission following an Indian Express report's revelation that Election Commissioners S S Sandhu and Vivek Joshi objected on record at least 14 times over the past 10 months to decisions concerning voter registration, deletions and restoration of names and the Special Intensive Revision of electoral rolls.\nThe Election Commission on Saturday asserted that all decisions relating to SIR received the unanimous approval of all three commissioners.\nIt also announced measures to assist voters facing notices during the revision",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-27T09:44:30"
    }
